<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Victor Hugo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1802">1802</a>–<a href="/years/1885">1885</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Victor Hugo was a French poet, novelist and dramatist, a leading figure of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanticism">Romanticism</a> and author of The Hunchback of Notre-Dame and Les Misérables. An opponent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a>'s coup of 1851, he went into exile, living in Jersey from 1852 until his expulsion in 1855 and then in Guernsey until 1870. He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> after the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">Second Empire</a> and was buried in the Panthéon.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/victor-hugo.jpg" alt="Victor Hugo" width="320" height="394" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Étienne Carjat · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Victor_Hugo_by_%C3%89tienne_Carjat_1876_-_full.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Victor-Marie Hugo was born on 26 February 1802 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/besancon">Besançon</a>, the youngest son of Joseph Léopold Sigisbert Hugo, a general in the Napoleonic army, and Sophie Trébuchet. His father was an atheist and an ardent supporter of the Republic, while his mother was loyal to the deposed dynasty. Because his father was an officer, the family moved often from posting to posting. In 1810 his father was made Count Hugo de Cogolludo y Sigüenza by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-bonaparte">Joseph Bonaparte</a>, then King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, though the Spanish title seems not to have been legally recognised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><p>In his youth Hugo resolved to be Chateaubriand or nothing, and his life came to parallel that of the older writer in many ways. Like him, he furthered the cause of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanticism">Romanticism</a>, became involved in politics and was forced into exile. He married Adèle Foucher in October 1822.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Hugo published his first novel, Hans of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iceland">Iceland</a>, in 1823 and his second, Bug-Jargal, in 1826. His five volumes of poetry between 1829 and 1840 cemented his reputation as one of the great elegiac and lyric poets of his time. The Hunchback of Notre-Dame appeared in 1831 and Les Misérables in 1862. He was at the forefront of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanticism">Romanticism</a> with his play Cromwell and his drama Hernani. After three unsuccessful attempts he was elected to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/academie-francaise">Académie française</a> in 1841, and he wrote the libretto of Louise Bertin's opera La Esmeralda in 1836.</p><p>Politically he moved from royalism toward republicanism. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-philippe-i">Louis Philippe I</a> appointed him a peer of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1845, where he spoke against the death penalty and social injustice and for freedom of the press. Elected in 1848 to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-assembly-of-france">National Assembly</a> of the Second Republic as a conservative, he broke with the conservatives in 1849 with a speech calling for an end to misery and poverty. His campaign to abolish the death penalty was known internationally.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>When Louis Napoleon seized complete power in 1851, Hugo openly declared him a traitor to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. He moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a>, then to Jersey, from which he was expelled for supporting the newspaper L'Homme. From October 1855 until 1870 he lived in self-imposed exile at Hauteville House in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-peter-port">Saint Peter Port</a>, Guernsey. In 1870 he returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, where he was hailed as a national hero. He lost a bid for re-election to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-assembly-of-france">National Assembly</a> in 1872 and was elected to the new Senate on 30 January 1876.</p><p>Hugo died on 22 May 1885, aged 83. He was given a state funeral in the Panthéon of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, attended by over two million people, the largest in French history. He produced more than 4,000 drawings, with about 3,000 surviving. His portrait was placed on French currency.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Victor Hugo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the nineteenth century it offered refuge to political exiles, among them <b>Victor Hugo</b>, who lived there from 1852 until his expulsion in 1855, and the actress Lillie Langtry, the Jersey Lily, carried its name into Victorian society.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bailiwick-of-jersey">Bailiwick of Jersey · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Caodaists hold that <b>Victor Hugo</b>, Sun Yat-sen and Trạng Trình, the Vietnamese Nostradamus, are the three main prophets of the Cao Đài pantheon, as a plaque at the entrance of the Tây Ninh Temple shows.</blockquote><a href="/religion/caodaism">Caodaism · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Victor Hugo</b>, in exile on Jersey from 1851 to 1855, took part in many sessions and came to believe he had contacted his late daughter Léopoldine and writers such as Shakespeare and Dante.</blockquote><a href="/religion/spiritism">Spiritism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Victor Hugo</b>’s preface to his play Cromwell of 1827 attacked the classical rules, and on 25 February 1830 the first night of his Hernani turned into a battle between young Romantics and defenders of the classical stage.</blockquote><a href="/movement/romanticism">Romanticism · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Victor Hugo</b> wrote the novel Notre-Dame de Paris, published in English as The Hunchback of Notre-Dame, in 1831 to save the cathedral; it was an enormous success and raised awareness of its decay.</blockquote><a href="/work/notre-dame-de-paris">Notre-Dame de Paris · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Writers as diverse as Henry Fielding and Naguib Mahfouz allude to the Nights by name, and among those it influenced are Jorge Luis Borges, Italo Calvino, Angela Carter, Gustave Flaubert, Goethe, <b>Victor Hugo</b>, Marcel Proust, Alexander Pushkin, Salman Rushdie, Leo Tolstoy and W. B. Yeats.</blockquote><a href="/work/one-thousand-and-one-nights">One Thousand and One Nights · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/besancon">Besançon</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.24,6.02&amp;year=1855" aria-label="Besançon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4691 506 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Victor Hugo"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5141 776h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5141 776h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=47.24,6.02&amp;year=1855">What was here in 1855? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;There is a spectacle more grand than the sea; it is heaven: there is a spectacle more grand than heaven; it is the inmost recesses of the soul.&#8221;</p><footer>Les Misérables (1862) &#183; tr. Isabel F. Hapgood, 1887 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/135/pg135-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The supreme happiness of life consists in the conviction that one is loved; loved for one’s own sake—let us say rather, loved in spite of one’s self…&#8221;</p><footer>Les Misérables (1862) &#183; tr. Isabel F. Hapgood, 1887 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/135/pg135-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;An invasion of armies can be resisted; an invasion of ideas cannot be resisted.&#8221;</p><footer>The History of a Crime (1877), conclusion &#183; tr. T. H. Joyce and Arthur Locker, 1877-78 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/10381/pg10381-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;If there is a culprit here, it is not my son—it is myself—it is I!—I, who for these last twenty-five years have opposed capital punishment—have contended for the inviolability of human life—have committed this crime, for which my son is now arraigned.&#8221;</p><footer>Speech in defence of his son, tried for an article against capital punishment, 1851 &#183; tr. in The World's Famous Orations, ed. William Jennings Bryan, 1906 (translator not named) &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_World%27s_Famous_Orations/Volume_7/In_Defense_of_His_Son" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…the right to criticize a law, and to criticize it severely—especially a penal law—is placed beside the duty of amelioration, like a torch beside the work under the artisan's hand.&#8221;</p><footer>Speech in defence of his son, tried for an article against capital punishment, 1851 &#183; tr. in The World's Famous Orations, ed. William Jennings Bryan, 1906 (translator not named) &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_World%27s_Famous_Orations/Volume_7/In_Defense_of_His_Son" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;This soul is full of shadow; sin is therein committed. The guilty one is not the person who has committed the sin, but the person who has created the shadow.&#8221;</p><footer>Les Misérables (1862), part 1, book 1 &#183; tr. Isabel F. Hapgood, 1887 &#183; spoken by Bishop Myriel &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/135/pg135-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
